    "md5": "9fb38371244f69ff93e5afeee53ac3db",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "Historically, the country has allocated a mere 0.12% of its annual GDP for research and development. Despite Sri Lanka\u2019s abundance of natural resources, there has been limited focus on utilising research to generate economic benefits. However, with the new Government in place, a significant number of local and international investors have shown keen interest in investing in Sri Lanka. The Government aims to capitalise on this momentum by efficiently managing investments to spur economic growth without delay.\n\nTo support this effort, the NIRDC has been established, chaired jointly by Secretary to the President Dr. Nandika Sanath Kumanayake and Senior Adviser to the President Prof. Gomika Udugamasooriya. The initiative includes a committee comprising experts from diverse fields such as scientific research, industry, economics, law, arts, and indigenous heritage.\n\nDuring the event, President Dissanayake highlighted that while human needs remain constant, the methods of fulfilling those needs evolve. He noted that innovation and new product development are key to shaping those new methods.\n\nAddressing past shortcomings, the President observed that Sri Lanka\u2019s outdated approach to technology and innovation has hindered its ability to compete in the global market. Despite technological advancements driving a shift in global market trends, Sri Lanka remains trapped in traditional sectors like tea, coconut, and rubber.\n\nPresident Dissanayake further underscored that globally leading companies have succeeded by integrating innovation with technology, noting that five out of the world\u2019s top 10 companies are technology-based.\n\nHe pointed out Sri Lanka\u2019s past neglect of technology as a key economic driver, contrasting it with developed countries that prioritised technological advancements and achieved significant economic growth."
  },
