  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-07-10-economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "num": "economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "date_str": "2025-07-10",
    "description": "Apparel makers seek 300-400-pct tax hike on Sri Lankans amid Trump tariffs",
    "url_metadata": "https://economynext.com/apparel-makers-seek-300-400-pct-tax-hike-on-sri-lankans-amid-trump-tariffs-229866/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "economynextcom",
    "time_ut": 1752163020.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-07-10-economynextcom-1aeced5e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "124c4f14c17bd246068fcb57435e99b0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1847,
    "chunk_text": "Apparel makers seek 300-400-pct tax hike on Sri Lankans amid Trump tariffs\n\nECONOMYNEXT \u2013 Sri Lanka\u2019s apparel makers are in discussions with the government to raise a para tariff on finished clothes by 300 to 400 to block access to foreign goods to local customers, an apparel official said.\n\nYashotharan Paramanantham, former Chairman of Sri Lanka Apparel Brand Association said they had fired the \u2018first shot\u2019 (wedi moo-ray) to stop online platforms from selling \u2018cheap\u2019 clothes to Sri Lankan customers and he was thankful for Customs to stop an earlier practice of charging by weight.\n\nThe domestic producers were supported by the Industries Ministry, he said.\n\nThe next step was to turn the guns on large importers who paid import taxes and VAT, by raising the cess.\n\nAsked by reporters how much the 300 to 400 percent increase in the para tariff sought by apparel brands was in rupee items, he said, the current import tax depended on HS code and it ranged from 75 rupees to 300 rupees per piece.\n\nAs a result 400 percent increase represented a tax of 300 to 1,200 rupees per item on the general public of Sri Lanka, giving apparel makers similar profits.\n\nSri Lanka follows Trump into \u2018de minimis\u2019 protectionism, China online platform hit\n\nSri Lanka looking at allowing non-commercial AliExpress style purchases for personal use\n\nSri Lanka can solve AliExpress crisis with Mangala Samaraweera de minimis rule: Harsha\n\nThe threat to increase the tax was made at a Sri Lanka Shippers Council media briefing where representatives of Sri Lanka Free Trade Manufacturers Association, the Joint Apparel Association Forum, and Sri Lanka Association of Exporters were present and watching.\n\nBefore this press conference, the Sri Lanka Shippers\u2019 Council was not known for protectionism but for promoting freer and frictionless trade."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-07-10-economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "num": "economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "date_str": "2025-07-10",
