    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6b64d3ea61b08d83db8e943c33217e8f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "The threat to increase the tax was made at a Sri Lanka Shippers Council media briefing where representatives of Sri Lanka Free Trade Manufacturers Association, the Joint Apparel Association Forum, and Sri Lanka Association of Exporters were present and watching.\n\nBefore this press conference, the Sri Lanka Shippers\u2019 Council was not known for protectionism but for promoting freer and frictionless trade.\n\nReporters pointed out that there was no logic in the argument of local apparel makers who demanded higher taxes on relatively poor customers in Sri Lanka who wanted \u2018cheap\u2019 clothes, while asking for tax reductions in the US or quota expansions in India.\n\nParamanantham, said the average global tax on apparel was higher than Sri Lanka. Local industries also faced a cess tax on the import of raw materials and fabric, he said.\n\nIt was pointed out that instead of demanding taxes on AliExpress customers and other Sri Lankans who used imported apparels at current para tariffs, the manufacturers should lobby to remove all para tariffs on imports so that they become export competitive.\n\nMisleading information was also given at the Shippers Council press conference that the IMF wanted import taxes as a revenue source. Import taxes are a source of revenue only as long as a product is not made in the country.\n\nIf the product is made locally the tax is pocketed by the domestic producer in a process known as \u2018tax arbitrage\u2019 and the government loses it, though the customer pays a price equal or close to the global+ price+plus+tax price.\n\nIt was pointed out that the IMF advocated phasing out para tariffs to make Sri Lanka an exporting nation that can compete by joining global value chains.\n\nHow protectionism kills exports is generally known now as the Lerner Symmetry Theorem, described in short as \u2018a tax on imports is a tax on exports.\u2019"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-07-10-economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "num": "economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "date_str": "2025-07-10",
    "description": "Apparel makers seek 300-400-pct tax hike on Sri Lankans amid Trump tariffs",
    "url_metadata": "https://economynext.com/apparel-makers-seek-300-400-pct-tax-hike-on-sri-lankans-amid-trump-tariffs-229866/",
    "lang": "en",
