    "time_ut": 1752163020.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-07-10-economynextcom-1aeced5e-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c7f8b8c10d58738a71a7bf4ff3f26ddc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1409,
    "chunk_text": "It was pointed out that the IMF advocated phasing out para tariffs to make Sri Lanka an exporting nation that can compete by joining global value chains.\n\nHow protectionism kills exports is generally known now as the Lerner Symmetry Theorem, described in short as \u2018a tax on imports is a tax on exports.\u2019\n\nIn the 19th century, British industrialists lobbied to cut taxes on imported foods (the Corn Laws) explaining that high grain prices in the UK led to higher wages and/or lower living standards for industrial workers and a clamour to raise wages, which in turn made their products expensive abroad.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, rice is taxed at 200 dollars a tonne (the import cost is only about 450 dollars) and maize is not only taxed, but is also under a license raj to give profits to farmers, landowners and collectors.\n\nCorn Laws were abolished by Prime Minister Robert Peel in 1846, who was defeated in parliament by the farmer/landowner lobby, but was immediately re-elected by the hungry people who were already hit by a potato blight.\n\nWilliam Cobden, an ardent free trader (and manufacturer) repeated the following statement said to have been made by a worker.\n\nIronically, unlike Sri Lanka\u2019s protectionist domestic apparel producers, the British free traders \u2013 including Cobden \u2013 were in textile manufacture. When food prices are high, it was explained that even domestic demand for garments fell."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-07-10-economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "num": "economynextcom-1aeced5e",
    "date_str": "2025-07-10",
    "description": "Apparel makers seek 300-400-pct tax hike on Sri Lankans amid Trump tariffs",
    "url_metadata": "https://economynext.com/apparel-makers-seek-300-400-pct-tax-hike-on-sri-lankans-amid-trump-tariffs-229866/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "economynextcom",
    "time_ut": 1752163020.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-07-10-economynextcom-1aeced5e-0003",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ddcca3e3029a06640e19b386b2178c2e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1824,
    "chunk_text": "Ironically, unlike Sri Lanka\u2019s protectionist domestic apparel producers, the British free traders \u2013 including Cobden \u2013 were in textile manufacture. When food prices are high, it was explained that even domestic demand for garments fell.\n\nSri Lanka industries however have not lobbied either to reduce their own para tariffs (large industries are in zones which are free of taxes) to build competitive SMEs, or advocated lower taxes on rice and more importantly maize to reduce protein malnutrition of the children of the poor or the living standards of their workers.\n\nBritish free traders had pointed out that food import taxes were promoted by the \u201cbread-taxing oligarchy, unprincipled, unfeeling, rapacious and plundering.\u201d\n\nIn Sri Lanka rent-seeking special interests that push up prices through import taxes or licenses are generally known as \u2018mafia\u2019.\n\nAfter the abolition of Corn Laws, Britain became an industrial power that has largely remained true to free trading principles to this day and it was not shaken even during Brexit.\n\nRELATED  Sri Lanka apparels to get duty free access to UK with Asian or African inputs\n\nA speaker at the media briefing also claimed that online platforms did not have local offices or create jobs.\n\nBut when imported goods are \u2018cheap\u2019 more money is left at the hands of consumers, who will buy more goods or non-traded services, creating jobs and entirely new businesses. The money may be spent on travel, food, eating out, entertainment, bank deposits, tuition fees, adding jobs and VAT revenues to the state.\n\nThat is why free trading countries prosper, have low taxes, have big domestic tourist sectors, and import labour, while those with protection and self-sufficiency like North Korea or Sri Lanka lag behind, have unemployment or export labour, analysts say."
  },
