    "chunk_text": "That is why free trading countries prosper, have low taxes, have big domestic tourist sectors, and import labour, while those with protection and self-sufficiency like North Korea or Sri Lanka lag behind, have unemployment or export labour, analysts say.\n\nIn addition to ending food import taxes, Prime Minister Peel also brought a strict law against the Bank of England, (and effectively made it difficult to start new note issuing banks) to reduce the central bank\u2019s independence to print money.\n\nLike in Sri Lanka, where macro-economists blame car imports (or oil or gold according to the fashion of the day, as forex shortages emerge after money is printed to cut rates) corn imports were blamed for monetary troubles by some British Mercantilists.\n\nPrime Minister Robert Peel however is said to have studied Adam Smith, David Ricardo (who was also a free trader despite being a landowner who profited from a high tax on grain imports) and also David Hume.\n\nModern central banks create forex shortages and external default primarily by rejecting Hume\u2019s price-specie-flow mechanism.\n\nForex shortages are another reason for protectionism and import substitution to \u2018save\u2019 foreign exchange.\n\n\u201cWhen imports were restricted during President Gotabaya Rajapaksa\u2019s time, due to foreign exchange problems,\u201d Paramanantham said. \u201cWe showed we could make all the clothes that were required in the country. New businesses were born.\n\n\u201cBut about 30 months ago the challenge came from online platforms like Temu and Ali baba (sic),\u201d he said.\n\nSimilar claims had been made by other domestic producers in the past including tile and ceramic makers, who sold factory rejects at special shops.(Colombo/July10/2025)"
